    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Benelli Showed the World Its New Tornado Naked Tre 903 Concept at EICMA 2026 and It's a Triple!\nTuned BMW 7 Series Comes With a Carbon Makeover, Looks Like a Massive Sports Sedan\n11 photos\nPhoto: Instagram | lartedesign\nIt’s been more than half a year since BMW pulled the wraps off the latest iteration 7 Series , and now the flagship sedan is getting some aftermarket attention courtesy of Larte Design.\nWhat would that be, you ask? Well, by touching it with its tuning stick, of course, which it just did. Or plans to do, because these are nothing more than mere renderings of the tuned project, yet unlike the usual digital illustrations that cross our screens, these actually preview something that is right around the corner. So, if you dig the design and plan to make your modern-day 7er look like this, then you know what to do, don’t you?\nBut speaking of the design, what is new? Well, unlike some other tuning jobs from different companies, which tend to keep things extremely discreet, Larte Design is not famously known for its restrained projects. And they weren’t going to make an exception for the latest iteration of the BMW 7 Series, which is a facelift of the G70 that hit the assembly line back in mid-2022, in case you forgot, and comes to life in Germany and in Southeast Asia.\nThe front hood has clearly come from the tuner’s portfolio. It features a pair of large vents in the middle, and two smaller ones on each side, and it is joined by a chunkier-looking splitter attached to the OEM bumper. These parts have been made of carbon fiber, and so have the side skirt attachments, the wing added on the trunk lid, and the large diffuser at the rear, which features a pair of integrated brake lights in the middle.\nPhoto: Instagram | lartedesign Do you know that no (visual) tuning job would be complete these days without a set of aftermarket wheels? Well, this BMW 7 Series also checks this box. Due to their multi-spoke design, the alloys remind us of Alpina’s creations, and that is definitely a compliment, though it is worth mentioning that Alpina’s wheels are better looking than these ones, this writer thinks.\nThe alloys have a black finish, sport a larger diameter than the stock ones, and had to be shod in rubber band tires in order to fit below the arches. The 7er in question appears to sit closer to the ground, so some suspension tweaks are part of the virtual (for now) makeover, and the car wears a red paint finish on the lower parts of the body, and a black look on the upper sections, whereas the add-ons mentioned above have a dry carbon look.\nWith Larte Design’s makeover, the latest iteration BMW 7 Series definitely looks a lot more aggressive than the standard one, but does this look suit it? You be the judge of that, and while you’re at it, you should know that the extras can also be painted black for a more discreet look. Speaking of painting, besides red, the tuner’s website also shows the car in other colors, including green, blue, gray, white, and so on.\nOne thing that the website, nor the social media post embedded at the bottom of the page, do not reveal is how much it will cost you in order to make your modern-day BMW 7 Series look like this. Therefore, if you really must go for this look, then you should contact the tuner and discuss the details. By the way, this body kit has been especially developed for the LCI (Life Cycle Impulse) iteration, which is Bimmer slang for facelift, version of the G70 7er.\nPhoto: Instagram | lartedesign According to Larte Design , every component of the body kit fits the factory mounting points, so the OEM-level fitment is guaranteed. This means that the installation should be a walk in the park, just like restoring the car back to its original condition. The tuner will ship the bundle worldwide, but realistically speaking, would you really want your 7er to look like this?\nBefore you decide whether you think it is worth it or not, we will remind you that the 2027 BMW 7 Series starts at $99,800 in the United States of America for the base 740, which uses a 394-horsepower 3-liter twin-turbocharged straight-six engine. The xDrive all-wheel drive system is a $3,000 optional extra in this case, and drops the 0 to 60 mph (0 to 97 kph) from 5.2 to 4.9 seconds, whereas the average fuel consumption is still rated at 30 mpg (7.8 l/100 km).\nThe latest BMW 7 Series features an updated front fascia and a new back end. The cockpit, meanwhile, looks basically the same as the ones belonging to other modern-day Bimmers, including the new X5, 3 Series, i3, etc. It is home to the Panoramic iDrive, a free-floating infotainment system, standard passenger display, a quirky-looking steering wheel with a four-spoke design, and a few other bits and bobs. View this post on Instagram A post shared by LARTE DESIGN | BODY KITS | WHEELS | AUTO DESIGN (@lartedesign)\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nBMW 7 Series BMW 7 series tuning Larte Design sedan Europe\nAbout the author: Cristian Gnaticov\nAfter a series of unfortunate events put an end to Cristian's dream of entering a custom built & tuned old-school Dacia into a rally competition, he moved on to drive press cars and write for a living. He's worked for several automotive online journals and now he's back at autoevolution after his first tour in the mid-2000s. Full profile\nUP NEXT Winningest Hemi Cuda Ever: Totaled in 1970, Won 45 Races in Five Years, You Never Heard of It",
